  │ Network down during auth     │ [38;5;153m{ success: false, error }[39m             │ nothing — no login happened             │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Auth returns 401/500         │ [38;5;153m{ success: false, error }[39m with status │ nothing                                 │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Auth OK, tracking POST fails │ [38;5;153m{ success: true, userId }[39m — logged in │ console warning only; [1mthe event is lost[0m │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mTRACKING_ENDPOINT[39m unset      │ [38;5;153m{ success: true, userId }[39m — logged in │ nothing, silently                       │
  └──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Row three is the deliberate trade you bought with fire-and-forget: login never suffers for tracking, and the price is
  that tracking is lossy. If you need guaranteed delivery instead, that's a queue-and-retry design and a different,
  larger conversation — say so now rather than after.

  [1m4. Testing[0m

  You chose no test tooling, so there is none, and I want that stated rather than implied: [1mthis change ships unverified
[0m  [1mby automated tests.[0m Verification is manual — open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, submit the form, confirm in devtools that the auth POST
  fires, that the tracking POST carries the right [38;5;153muserId[39m, and that killing the tracking endpoint still logs you in. I
  can't run that for you in this environment, so I'll report exactly what I did and didn't check.

  [1mThree things I want you to push back on if I've got them wrong[0m

  - [1m[38;5;153mtimestamp[39m is the client's clock[0m, so it's user-controllable and skewable. For real tracking the server should stamp
    arrival time and treat the client value as a hint. I'm sending it anyway because you asked for it in the record, but
    I'd weight the server's.
  - [1mCredentials now actually leave the browser.[0m The stub never did. [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is https so it's encrypted, but it's
    [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m — a placeholder. Pointing real passwords at a domain you don't control is the kind of thing worth a
    second look before this runs anywhere real.
  - [1mI'm keeping [38;5;153muser: username[39m in the return[0m alongside the new [38;5;153muserId[39m, purely so the existing shape doesn't break. If
    nothing outside this repo consumes it, dropping it is tidier — your call.

  Does this look right? I'll write it to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-tracking-design.md[39m once you approve,
  then run the spec review gate before you read it.

